[s0l0!-header][h1]Functions in C[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

[b]Functions [/b]are central to C programming and are used to accomplish a program solution as a series of subtasks. 
By now you know that every C program contains a [b]main[/b]() function. And you're familiar with the [b]printf() [/b]function. 

You can also create your own functions. 
A function:
 • is a block of code that performs a specific task
 • is reusable
 • makes a program easier to test
 • can be modified without changing the calling program

Even a simple program is easier to understand when [b]main()[/b] is broken down into subtasks that are implemented with functions. 
For example, it's clear that the goal of this program is to calculate the square of a number: [code format="c" ]int main() {
  int x, result;
  
  x = 5;
  result = [b]square[/b](x);
  printf("%d squared is %d\n", x, result);
    
  return 0;
}[/code]
In order to use the [b]square [/b]function, we need to declare it. 
Declarations usually appear above the [b]main()[/b] function and take the form: [code format="raw" ]return_type function_name(parameters); [/code]
The [b]return_type [/b]is the type of value the function sends back to the calling statement. The [b]function_name [/b]is followed by parentheses. Optional [b]parameter [/b]names with type declarations are placed inside the parentheses.
[note]A function is not required to return a value, but a return type must still be in the declaration. In this case, the keyword [b]void [/b]is used. 
For example, the [b]display_message [/b]function declaration indicates the function does not return a value: [b]void display_message();[/b][/note]

