[s0l0!-header][h1]Functions in C[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

When the parameter types and names are included in a declaration, the declaration is called a [b]function prototype[/b].

For example, the [b]square [/b]function prototype appears above main(): [code format="c" ]#include <stdio.h>

/* declaration */
[b]int square (int num);[/b] 

int main() {
  int x, result;
  
  x = 5;
  result = square(x);
  printf("%d squared is %d\n", x, result);
    
  return 0;
} [/code]
Our [b]square [/b]function returns an integer and takes one parameter of type int.

The last step is actually [b]defining [/b]the function. Function definitions usually appear after the [b]main()[/b] function. 
The complete program below shows the [b]square [/b]function declaration and definition: [code format="c" codeId="1662"]#include <stdio.h>

/* declaration */
[b]int square (int num); [/b]

int main() {
  int x, result;
  
  x = 5;
  result = square(x);
  printf("%d squared is %d\n", x, result);
    
  return 0;
}

/* definition */
[b]int square (int num) {
  int y;

  y = num * num;

  return(y);
}[/b] [/code]
As you can see, the [b]square [/b]function calculates and returns the square of its parameter.
A function can take multiple parameters - in this case they must be separated by commas.
The [b]return [/b]statement is used to send a value back to the calling statement.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

