[s0l0!-header][h1]Function Parameters[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

A function's [b]parameters [/b]are used to receive values required by the function. Values are passed to these parameters as [b]arguments [/b]through the function call. 
By default, arguments are passed by value, which means that a copy of data is given to the parameters of the called function. The actual variable isn't passed into the function, so it won't change.
Arguments passed to a function are matched to parameters by position. Therefore, the first argument is passed to the first parameter, the second to the second parameter, and so on.
The following program demonstrates parameters passed by value: [code format="c" codeId="1663"]#include <stdio.h>

int sum_up (int x, int y); 

int main() {
  int x, y, result;
  
  x = 3;
  y = 12;
  result = sum_up(x, y);
  printf("%d + %d = %d", x, y, result);
    
  return 0;
}

int sum_up (int x, int y) { 
  x += y;
  return(x);
} [/code]
The program output is: 3 + 12 = 15
The values of [b]x[/b] and [b]y[/b] were passed to [b]sum_up[/b]. Note that even though the value of parameter [b]x[/b] was changed in [b]sum_up[/b], the value of argument [b]x[/b] in main() was not changed because only its value was passed into the parameter [b]x[/b].
[note]The parameters in a function declaration are the [b]formal parameters[/b]. The values passed to these parameters are the arguments, sometimes called the [b]actual parameters[/b].[/note]

