[s0l0!-header][h1]Variable Scope[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

[b]Variable scope[/b] refers to the visibility of variables within a program. 
Variables declared in a function are [b]local [/b]to that block of code and cannot be referred to outside the function. 
Variables declared outside all functions are [b]global [/b]to the entire program. 
For example, constants declared with a [b]#define[/b] at the top of a program are visible to the entire program.
The following program uses both [b]local [/b]and [b]global [/b]variables: [code format="c" codeId="1664"]#include <stdio.h>

[b]int global1 = 0; [/b]

int main() {    
[b]  int local1, local2;[/b]
  
  local1 = 5;
  local2 = 10;
  global1 = local1 + local2;
  printf("%d \n", global1);  /* 15 */
    
  return 0;
} [/code]
When arguments are passed to function parameters, the parameters act as local variables. Upon exiting a function, parameters and any local variables in the function are destroyed.
[note]Use global variables with caution. They should be initialized before using to avoid unexpected results. And because they can be changed anywhere in a program, global variables can lead to hard to detect errors.[/note]

